WEBVTT

00:00:00.675 --> 00:00:19.523
<v Casey>Hey, welcome back. Today we've got Carl Olsen's fight to stop a highway expansion through Goldstream Park — an Elder holding B.C. to a promise it made him years ago. We're using it to ask the real question underneath: who actually gets to say no and have it stick, with or without a title decision behind them. That and more, coming right up.

00:00:35.498 --> 00:00:55.402
<v Casey>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities — I'm Casey. It's Thursday, August 20th, 2026. Speaking to you from the traditional territories of the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh nations here in the Cariboo region. Today's theme is Indigenous Lands &amp; Innovation.

00:00:56.402 --> 00:01:06.298
<v Riley>And I'm Riley. We're looking at how nations here are using land stewardship and technology on their own terms, not as an add-on bolted onto someone else's plan.

00:01:06.398 --> 00:01:24.942
<v Riley>Quick weather sweep — Horsefly Lake is 13 degrees this morning, mainly clear, high of 27, overcast moving in tomorrow. 100 Mile House at 15 and clear, Williams Lake at 14, Quesnel at 12, and out on the Chilcotin plateau, Anahim Lake sitting at 10 with clear skies.

00:01:25.356 --> 00:01:28.812
<v Casey>Good day for it. Let's get into what's happening across the region.

00:01:30.212 --> 00:01:45.764
<v Casey>Williams Lake's Cariboo Memorial Recreation Complex is going into its annual maintenance shutdown starting August 24th. Pool, ice surface, and the mechanical systems underneath both — all offline for the stretch of work that keeps the building running the rest of the year.

00:01:46.910 --> 00:01:51.990
<v Riley>Check the Tribune's site for the reopening date if you had a swim planned that week.

00:01:53.331 --> 00:02:16.483
<v Riley>MLA Lorne Doerkson has an op-ed in the Tribune arguing B.C. needs to be better equipped to respond to forest fires. He represents Cariboo-Chilcotin, and he's writing after a summer that tested exactly the capacity he's talking about — equipment, crew numbers, response funding, not evacuation policy. He doesn't put a dollar figure on it, but the ask is clearly aimed at next year's budget cycle.

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:23.556
<v Casey>Op-eds like that tend to get cited either way once the budget lands — proof something changed, or proof it didn't.

00:02:24.837 --> 00:02:35.525
<v Casey>A vehicle fire in Quesnel made local news yesterday. My Cariboo Now reported no injuries — one more entry in a summer where dry conditions are stacking risk onto more than just timber stands.

00:02:36.724 --> 00:02:56.700
<v Riley>In the 100 Mile Free Press, columnist Kristin Buxton wrote about wildfire's toll on mental health — not property loss or evacuation logistics, but the fatigue of repeated low-grade emergency. People who've lived through three or four fire seasons in a row describe it differently than people going through their first one.

00:02:57.328 --> 00:03:01.224
<v Casey>A cost that never shows up in any damage assessment or insurance claim.

00:03:01.724 --> 00:03:20.884
<v Casey>A tariff deal is reportedly on the horizon for B.C. forestry, though the outlook is still described as uncertain. Mills here have operated under punishing American duties for years, and even a partial resolution matters in towns where the mill is the largest single employer. The Black Press piece doesn't name a percentage or a timeline.

00:03:22.118 --> 00:03:34.302
<v Riley>Nobody in Quesnel or Williams Lake is celebrating "on the horizon" until there's an actual signed number. But after four straight years of "no deal," any softening in the language counts as news on its own.

00:03:35.202 --> 00:04:05.562
<v Riley>The Narwhal has a longer piece on Carl Olsen, a W̱SÁNEĆ Elder once arrested for hunting on his own territory, now holding the B.C. government to a promise it made him years ago. He's fighting a highway expansion through the Malahat that would cut into Goldstream Park, and he describes himself as trying to be the voice for the salmon that spawn in the creek there. The province apparently promised a consultation process before any expansion decision was locked in — Olsen's position is that the sequencing has already been skipped.

00:04:06.812 --> 00:04:13.980
<v Casey>We'll come back to that in the Deep Dive — it's a clean example of a fight happening without the legal backing a title decision would give it.

00:04:15.482 --> 00:04:29.186
<v Casey>Ontario is reopening its sandhill crane hunt. The birds were listed as threatened not long ago; the population has recovered enough that farmers are now reporting real crop losses, and the province decided regulated hunting was the appropriate response.

00:04:29.785 --> 00:04:42.289
<v Riley>Noah Smith's newsletter, Noahpinion, argues economists are too quick to dismiss falling birth rates as a manageable problem, pushing back specifically against economist Daron Acemoglu's more relaxed framing.

00:04:43.731 --> 00:04:51.051
<v Casey>Rural B.C. school districts have been living that math for two decades — ask any district that's closed a wing for the answer already.

00:04:52.581 --> 00:05:09.541
<v Casey>NPR is covering hospital worker strikes in the U.S., where pay is only part of the dispute — workers are citing patient violence as a driving issue. The reporting cites hospital staff being seven times more likely to be injured on the job through violent incidents than the general working population.

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:17.786
<v Riley>Seven times. That's not a morale complaint, that's a staffing and security failure showing up as a labor dispute.

00:05:19.303 --> 00:05:31.375
<v Riley>ScienceAlert reports that people who live past a hundred tend to carry an unusual abundance of a specific immune cell — killer T cells — which researchers think may partly explain the longevity.

00:05:32.689 --> 00:05:50.961
<v Casey>And Business Insider is covering a study finding U.S. Air Force, Navy, and Coast Guard veterans face higher rates of ALS than veterans from other branches. The study flags possible service-related exposures as a factor but doesn't identify a specific cause — an open question the researchers are still chasing, not a settled finding.

00:05:52.450 --> 00:06:03.130
<v Casey>Nautilus has a lighter one — researchers tracked down a blind, pale, worm-like fish species that had been notoriously hard to locate in the wild, with an assist from footage circulating on TikTok.

00:06:03.737 --> 00:06:07.273
<v Riley>Crowdsourced ichthyology. Didn't expect to say that sentence today.

00:06:08.989 --> 00:06:23.453
<v Riley>ScienceDaily is covering a machine-learning breakthrough — researchers used AI to design "intrabodies," modified antibodies engineered to function inside human cells, which could open new treatment paths for Alzheimer's, Parkinson's, and motor neuron disease.

00:06:23.993 --> 00:06:27.569
<v Casey>Early-stage lab work. Not something to bank on for a while yet.

00:06:28.269 --> 00:06:42.301
<v Casey>The Atlantic reports that Marc Rosenblum, the statistician who ran the U.S. government's immigration data operation, has resigned from Homeland Security. Former colleagues believe it's tied to a broader effort to reduce transparency around enforcement numbers.

00:06:43.566 --> 00:06:54.102
<v Riley>Not a vote we get in Canada, but it matters for the precedent — what happens to public accountability once a government stops publishing the numbers that would hold it to account.

00:06:55.580 --> 00:07:20.876
<v Riley>Last one for the roundup — The Tyee has a piece on Alberta quietly expanding two-tier health care. A law that received royal assent back in December now permits private-pay diagnostic testing, letting people who can afford it skip the public wait list entirely. The Tyee's framing is blunt — they describe it as a plan to unwind public health care, not just in Alberta but as a template other provinces might eventually copy.

00:07:22.032 --> 00:07:37.008
<v Casey>It isn't law here in B.C. But it's exactly the kind of policy that travels once one province tests how much pushback it draws — worth a look the next time it surfaces in a provincial budget debate. That's the roundup — onto something with a little more warmth to it.

00:07:38.407 --> 00:08:05.295
<v Riley>Today's spotlight is on Denisiqi Services Society, which has provided culturally grounded child and family services for Tŝilhqot'in and Ulkatcho communities for more than twenty years now. This isn't a two-year pilot funded off a grant cycle. It's a standing institution that communities built and kept running because they insisted on controlling how it operated, generation to generation. You can learn more at denisiqi dot org.

00:08:05.856 --> 00:08:11.904
<v Casey>Twenty years of continuity in child and family services is rare on its own, and it deserves the mention just for that.

00:08:12.519 --> 00:08:17.567
<v Riley>It does. And it's a useful frame heading into the conversation we're about to have.

00:08:18.967 --> 00:08:32.679
<v Casey>An entire industry that once described itself as building the future has started describing its own work as pointless — layoffs, AI anxiety, the collapse of a mission-driven self-image that carried people through two decades of eighty-hour weeks.

00:08:33.239 --> 00:08:53.015
<v Riley>The Goldstream Park story is a strange mirror held up to that. Carl Olsen isn't burned out. He's been fighting the same fight for years — arrested once for hunting on his own territory, now decades later holding the province to a promise on a highway expansion — and by every account in that piece, he hasn't lost faith in the work.

00:08:53.563 --> 00:09:06.875
<v Casey>Because the work was never a mission statement handed down from a board. It's his territory, his relationship to a specific stretch of salmon-bearing creek. There's no all-hands meeting where leadership quietly decides the vision has changed.

00:09:07.445 --> 00:09:27.629
<v Riley>Tech's crisis of meaning is partly a crisis of borrowed purpose. Workers were told their code was changing the world, and when the stock price wobbled, the purpose evaporated along with the valuation. Governance tied to land doesn't have that failure mode built in — the consequence of the fight is the point, not the story told about it.

00:09:28.129 --> 00:09:44.233
<v Casey>Except romanticizing that comparison misses how exhausting the actual position is. Olsen has had to personally keep showing up for years because a government promise wasn't self-enforcing. That's one person compensating, at real personal cost, for a structural failure nobody else is fixing.

00:09:44.806 --> 00:10:04.254
<v Riley>Compensating for a structural failure in defense of something real is a different kind of exhausting than performing purpose for a company that changes strategy every eighteen months. One is hard because the stakes are real. The other is hard because the stakes were never real to begin with, and eventually people notice.

00:10:04.773 --> 00:10:15.237
<v Casey>Both exhaust the person doing the work regardless of which category it falls into. The actual question is who's supposed to be sharing the load instead of leaving it on one Elder's shoulders.

00:10:15.837 --> 00:10:46.085
<v Riley>The governance question underneath it is who holds the authority, so it isn't riding on one person indefinitely. The Tŝilhqot'in title decision from 2014 answers that differently than Olsen's situation does. That ruling didn't grant consultation rights, it recognized court-backed decision-making authority over a defined territory. Twelve years on, that's a legal floor Olsen's fight doesn't have — Goldstream sits inside a provincial park on Crown land, not adjudicated title.

00:10:46.715 --> 00:11:04.475
<v Casey>Which tells you something about why he's still out there arguing case by case. Without the floor Tŝilhqot'in has, W̱SÁNEĆ leadership is negotiating on the strength of a broken promise and whatever public pressure they can generate — a materially weaker position, however committed the person holding it happens to be.

00:11:05.070 --> 00:11:16.438
<v Riley>Weaker, yes, but the mechanism still moved something. Public pressure is what got the province to make the initial commitment in the first place, years before this expansion fight started.

00:11:16.338 --> 00:11:19.498
<v Casey>Moved a commitment on paper. Hasn't moved the highway route yet.

00:11:20.095 --> 00:11:24.359
<v Riley>Not yet. That part's still open, and the Narwhal piece doesn't say how it resolves.

00:11:24.945 --> 00:11:53.569
<v Casey>The harder version of the tech comparison is this — knowledge workers lost faith because their sense of purpose turned out to be contingent on a stock price they never controlled. Indigenous governance fights are contingent too, on court rulings, on which ministry holds the file this budget cycle, on provincial goodwill that can evaporate with an election. The real difference isn't that one side is durable and the other isn't. It's that one side never assumed the system was on their side to begin with.

00:11:54.117 --> 00:12:15.637
<v Riley>Nobody doing Indigenous governance work described the relationship with the Crown as a mission they trusted going in. The fight has always assumed bad faith as a starting condition, which means there's no illusion left to lose when a government backslides on a commitment. Tech workers are only discovering now what that starting condition feels like.

00:12:16.275 --> 00:12:38.067
<v Casey>And that starting condition produces a different kind of durability — not optimism, vigilance. Denisiqi Services didn't survive twenty years because anyone trusted the funding relationship to hold steady on its own. It survived because the community insisted on controlling the governance structure, so the organization's continuity didn't depend on any single ministry's ongoing goodwill.

00:12:38.466 --> 00:13:00.042
<v Riley>Denisiqi's staying power isn't primarily a funding story, it's a governance story — the board answers to the communities it serves, not to whichever grant officer is reviewing the file that year. Compare that to how most tech companies describe their own mission — a values statement authored by executives, revisable the moment the board composition changes.

00:13:00.692 --> 00:13:07.748
<v Casey>One structure answers to the people who bear the actual consequences of failure. The other answers to shareholders who mostly never will.

00:13:08.283 --> 00:13:30.355
<v Riley>Which is close to the real answer for why tech's mission-driven identity collapsed as fast as it did once the layoffs started. It was never an actual governance structure to begin with — it was a marketing frame layered over ordinary corporate decision-making, and ordinary corporate decision-making optimizes for the market's mood, not for the mission it claims to serve.

00:13:30.855 --> 00:13:59.423
<v Casey>The NCARB architecture licensure report sitting in today's material makes this uncomfortable in a different direction — more diversity showing up in the candidate pool, persistent gaps in who actually gets licensed and ends up holding real authority once inside the profession. It's the same pattern we've flagged before with Indigenous-led project delivery running without the underlying governance authority behind it: more participation on paper isn't the same thing as more decision-making power.

00:13:59.989 --> 00:14:19.837
<v Riley>What's different today is that Goldstream shows the reverse failure mode. Olsen has standing, a public commitment on record, decades of relationship with the file. What he doesn't have is anything like Tŝilhqot'in's court-recognized title. Different gap, same underlying question — who actually gets to say no and have it stick.

00:14:20.431 --> 00:14:33.239
<v Casey>And "who gets to say no" is a cleaner test than anything framed around mission or meaning. Tŝilhqot'in can say no and have a court behind that no. Olsen can say no and hope the province still feels bound by what it said years ago.

00:14:33.856 --> 00:14:51.832
<v Riley>Which argues for building the legal floor before the crisis lands, not scrambling to build it during the fight. A nation without a title decision is negotiating from public pressure alone every single time a dispute comes up, and that's a much heavier lift per fight than having the floor already in place.

00:14:52.531 --> 00:15:14.451
<v Casey>I'd push back on the legal-floor framing as a general solution. Tŝilhqot'in title took decades of litigation, real money, and a specific set of facts about occupation and use that not every nation can replicate on the same timeline. Xatśūll's history with the Crown looks different. Williams Lake First Nation's looks different again. Title isn't a template you photocopy onto the next dispute.

00:15:15.006 --> 00:15:43.198
<v Riley>No, but the underlying mechanism is transferable even where the destination isn't identical — documented occupation, continuous use, oral history treated as evidence rather than folklore in a courtroom. Monitoring technology, land-use records, structured oral history capture — all of that builds the evidentiary record a future title claim would eventually need, on the community's own terms, starting now rather than waiting for a crisis to force the question.

00:15:43.750 --> 00:15:57.614
<v Casey>That's a multi-decade investment for a payoff that isn't guaranteed to arrive on any particular timeline. Meanwhile the highway gets built, the park gets logged, and the immediate fight still has to be won with whatever tools are on hand today, this year.

00:15:58.258 --> 00:16:05.170
<v Riley>True. It isn't a substitute for the fight in front of Olsen right now. It's groundwork for the fight after this one.

00:16:05.670 --> 00:16:35.398
<v Casey>Which loops the tech-sadness question back with a sharper edge. The knowledge workers feeling adrift right now are discovering, late and expensively, that meaning tied only to a company's own self-image was never load-bearing in the first place. Indigenous governance work has always built its meaning on something with actual legal and physical weight attached — land, water, a specific salmon run with a name and a season. It's that meaning needs an anchor sitting outside the institution telling you what your work is supposed to mean.

00:16:36.032 --> 00:17:00.792
<v Riley>I'd frame the anchor as accountability rather than meaning as such. Tŝilhqot'in governance answers to the community that holds the title. A tech company answers to a board that can rewrite the mission statement in a single quarter if the numbers demand it. The durability isn't about feeling connected to the work — it's about who actually has the power to revoke your authority to keep doing it.

00:17:01.334 --> 00:17:09.718
<v Casey>Fine, accountability instead of meaning. Same landing point either way — it has to sit somewhere other than a company's own communications department.

00:17:10.317 --> 00:17:42.413
<v Riley>Concretely, for a listener here in the Cariboo wondering what any of this looks like on the ground — a community group doesn't need to wait for a title case to start building its own record. Oral history capture, land-use documentation, a monitoring program that logs data the community itself owns and controls rather than handing it to a consultant. Denisiqi's twenty years applies the same principle to child and family services: build the governance structure first, and let programs sit inside that structure rather than the other way around.

00:17:43.016 --> 00:17:57.120
<v Casey>And ask, before any new program launches, who can shut it down and who it actually answers to day to day. If the honest answer is "a ministry, annually, depending on that year's budget," that's the same authority gap wearing a different name on the letterhead.

00:17:57.750 --> 00:18:26.126
<v Riley>(thoughtful) The real question to carry out of this isn't whether the technology exists or the funding shows up this cycle — it's whether the decision-making already sits with the community before the crisis hits, or gets negotiated for the first time in the middle of it. And on that note — if you've got a story tip or a project like this happening in your own community, send it to feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a. We'd want to hear about it before the next time this comes up.

00:18:26.704 --> 00:18:41.872
<v Casey>Olsen's still out there negotiating in the middle of it, on the strength of a promise nobody's written into law — that's the position nobody actually wants to be stuck defending, Elder or otherwise. Send us the Cariboo examples we're missing, and have a good rest of your Thursday.

00:18:42.052 --> 00:18:42.820
<v Riley>Take care out there.

00:18:43.467 --> 00:18:56.563
<v Casey>Look, I'll be straight with you — we didn't get First Nations comms folks in the room for this one, and that's on us. If you're working in Indigenous communications in the Cariboo and you've got thoughts on this, we'd actually like to hear them.